.TH UNLINK 2 "May 22, 1985"
.UC 4
.SH NAME
unlink, unlinkat_beneath \- remove directory entry
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.ft B
#include <unistd.h>
.PP
.ft B
int unlink(const char *path);
.PP
.ft B
int unlinkat_beneath(int rootfd, const char *path, int expected_fd);
.fi
.ft R
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Unlink
removes the entry for the file
.I path
from its directory.
If this entry was the last link to the file,
and no process has the file open, then
all resources associated with the file are reclaimed.
If, however, the file was open in any process, the actual
resource reclamation is delayed until it is closed,
even though the directory entry has disappeared.
.PP
.I Unlinkat_beneath
starts at the directory referred to by
.I rootfd
and accepts only a nonempty relative path whose components differ from
``.'' and ``..''.
It follows neither symbolic links nor mounted-file-system transitions.
The call removes the final entry only when the locked lookup result is the
same regular file retained by
.I expected_fd.
The directory descriptor, expected descriptor, and resolved entry must remain
valid for the duration of the call.
.SH "RETURN VALUE"
Upon successful completion, a value of 0 is returned.
Otherwise, a value of \-1 is returned and
.I errno
is set to indicate the error.
.SH "ERRORS"
The calls fail when:
.TP 15
[ENOTDIR]
A component of the path prefix is not a directory.
.TP 15
[EINVAL]
The pathname contains a character with the high-order bit set.
.TP 15
[ENAMETOOLONG]
A component of a pathname exceeded 255 characters,
or an entire path name exceeded 1023 characters.
.TP 15
[ENOENT]
The named file does not exist.
.TP 15
[EBADF]
.I Unlinkat_beneath
was given an invalid directory or expected-file descriptor.
.TP 15
[ENOTDIR]
.I Rootfd
does not refer to a directory.
.TP 15
[EINVAL]
.I Expected_fd
does not refer to a regular file, or the restricted path contains an empty,
``.'', or ``..'' component.
.TP 15
[ESTALE]
The final entry does not refer to the regular file retained by
.I expected_fd.
.TP 15
[EXDEV]
The restricted lookup would cross a mounted-file-system boundary.
.TP 15
[EACCES]
Search permission is denied for a component of the path prefix.
.TP 15
[EACCES]
Write permission is denied on the directory containing the link
to be removed.
.TP 15
[ELOOP]
Too many symbolic links were encountered in translating the pathname.
.TP 15
[EPERM]
The named file is a directory.
.TP 15
[EPERM]
The directory containing the file is marked sticky,
and neither the containing directory nor the file to be removed
are owned by the effective user ID.
.TP 15
[EBUSY]
The entry to be unlinked is the mount point for a
mounted file system.
.TP 15
[EIO]
An I/O error occurred while deleting the directory entry
or deallocating the inode.
.TP 15
[EROFS]
The named file resides on a read-only file system.
.TP 15
[EFAULT]
.I Path
points outside the process's allocated address space.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
close(2), link(2), openat(2), rmdir(2)
